@node "intro" "Introduction"

@{u}@{b}Introduction@{ub}@{uu}

prism2.device is a SANA-II network driver for 11Mbps wireless Ethernet cards
based on the Prism II chipset and variants. It supports PCMCIA and
Compact Flash (CF) cards in the A1200/A600, and PCI cards in all AmigaOS 4
systems and in PCs running Amithlon.

This version of prism2.device supports WPA and WPA2 encryption, including
both TKIP and CCMP/AES encryption modes. However, not all hardware supports
all encryption modes (see below for details). The driver also supports
unencrypted networks and the old WEP encryption standard if needed.

@{u}@{b}Card Types@{ub}@{uu}

A number of related card types are supported by the driver. They can be
categorised according to their chipset and firmware into the following
groups.

@{b}Prism II@{ub}

This category includes all cards based on true Prism II, 2.5 and 3 chipsets
that use Intersil firmware.

Advantages:
  - Supports WPA/WPA2 encryption (both TKIP and CCMP/AES).

Disadvantages:
  - WPA/WPA2 encryption is not hardware accelerated.
  - Firmware upgrade must be performed on a PC in order to support WPA/WPA2.

@{b}Hermes@{ub}

These cards use Lucent/Agere firmware. They are only available in the PCMCIA
form factor, and are recognisable by their white pin connectors.

Advantages:
  - Supports hardware-accelerated WPA encryption (TKIP only).
  - No requirement to upgrade firmware on a PC for WPA support.

Disadvantages:
  - WPA2 encryption (CCMP/AES) is not supported.
  - Cannot connect to some modern routers due to a firmware bug.
  - Takes a few seconds to load firmware from disk when started on an
    unaccelerated Amiga.

@{b}Hermes II@{ub}

These later generation Hermes cards are similar in functionality to the
original, but have the additional disadvantage that they may cause a crash
if an attempt is made to connect to a modern router. However, once you have
found a router that works with the card, that problem should not matter.

In terms of appearance, they have the same white pin connector but a
smaller antenna.

@endnode


@node "compatibility" "Card Compatibility"

@{u}@{b}Card Compatibility@{ub}@{uu}

The table below shows compatibility information for prism2.device with a
variety of wireless cards. For cards not listed below, you will have to do
some research. If a card's chipset is stated as Orinoco, Lucent, Agere,
Prism II or Prism 2.5 @{u}and@{uu} its bus type is PCMCIA, CF or PCI, it is likely to
work. Some Prism 3 cards also work if they have a flash memory. Note that a
few otherwise-compatible PCMCIA cards are 3.3V-only and therefore
incompatible with the A1200/A600 (such cards will not fit in the computer's
slot if they are designed properly). If the card doesn't match these
requirements, it will definitely @{u}not@{uu} work.

All compatible PCI cards use Intersil firmware and therefore support WEP,
TKIP and CCMP/AES. Lucent/Agere PCMCIA cards can be identified by their
white pin connectors.

